  "query_preview": "..\nToday: Winds remain breezy along the Cascade gaps early this\nmorning in the wake of a shortwave trough that made its way\nacross the region. Red Flag conditions will continue through \nthe early morning hours for the east slopes of the Cascades, \nwhere latest observations still show winds gusting between 25-40\nmph in spots. While winds will continue to ease through the \nmorning hours, expect another round of breezy westerly winds to\ndevelop this afternoon and evening as another shortwave traverses\nthe region. This will again lead to elevated to locally critical\nfire weather conditions in the Wenatchee area and the Waterville\nPlateau with gusts around 20-35 mph and relative humidities \nprimarily between 15-25 percent. The wildcard remains smoke \nreducing temperatures in those areas, which would help reduce \nfire risk. Elsewhere across the area, expect temperatures to \ncool a few degrees today under the influence of zonal flow aloft\nand another passing shortwave. Wildfire smoke and haze will continue\nto impact the region, especially in the vicinity of or downwind\nof ongoing wildfires.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Zonal flow will continue through at least \nTuesday, bringing near normal temperatures to the region and \nbreezy winds to the Cascade gaps in the late afternoons and \nevenings. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: Confidence in the forecast decreases \nheading into the second half of the week as a broad shortwave \ntrough sinks down from British Columbia. This system may bring the \nfirst chance of precipitation in a few weeks to northern areas near \nthe Canadian border by Wednesday. While there is a 15-25% chance\nof showers, preciptiation looks to remain rather limited with \nthis system. Small chances of (10-20%) of thunderstorms remain\npossible in northeast WA and northern ID both Wednesday and \nThursday afternoon and evenings. Quasi-zonal flow will then \nbriefly return on Friday as the trough to the north exits the \narea. Ensemble guidance remains indicative of an upper level \ntrough developing along the coast over the weekend and bringing\nsouthwest flow aloft to the area. This could continue to raise \nfurther concerns for thunderstorms across the region by the \nweekend.",
